"""
## Purpose and audience
For the Engine2D Vulkan maintainer who touches the image src-over kernel:
`_glsl_blit()` in `backend_vulkan_glsl.spl` and the `spirv_blit()` words in
`backend_vulkan_spirv_raster_blobs.spl`. Companion to
`doc/08_tracking/bug/vulkan_glsl_blend_formula_stale_vs_cpu_2026-09-12.md`.

The frozen CPU oracle is `blend()` in `src/lib/**/engine2d/color.spl`: it
composites in premultiplied space and unpremultiplies by the resulting output
alpha, so `dst_weight = (da * (255 - sa)) / 255` appears BOTH in the alpha
accumulation and in every channel numerator. The pre-unpremultiply formula that
this file exists to keep out uses `inv` in the channel numerators and divides by
the constant 255. For `0x01020304` over `0x10203040` the two disagree by a
readable amount -- `0x101E2D3C` (frozen) vs `0x101F2F3F` (stale) -- and until
2026-09-12 the checked-in GLSL text carried the stale one while the hand-
assembled SPIR-V blob carried the frozen one, with nothing in the tree able to
notice the divergence.

## Operator workflow
`SIMPLE_LIB=src SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpreter SIMPLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=0 \
 <vulkan-capable simple> run \
 test/01_unit/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/engine2d/backend_vulkan_blend_cpu_parity_spec.spl`

The byte-level companion is `sh scripts/check/check-blit-spirv-pinned.shs`,
which proves the committed blob IS glslangValidator output for the committed
GLSL. The two are complementary and neither replaces the other: the pin check
proves the device runs the text you can read, this file proves the text is
RIGHT. A GLSL-only sabotage is invisible to the device until the blob is
regenerated -- that asymmetry is the whole bug, and it is why the sabotage
recipe below has a regeneration step in the middle of it.

## Compatibility and limitations
REQUIRES a real Vulkan device and says so by FAILING, never skipping: the first
expectation asserts `backend_name() == "vulkan"`. `draw_image_blend` has a host
fallback (`emu_draw_image_blend`) that computes the CPU `blend()` directly, so a
skip -- or a silent fallback -- would turn every exact-value claim here into a
tautology about the emulator.

Covers the two branches the kernel actually has for a src-over composite: the
translucent path (`0 < sa < 255`, the arithmetic), and the `sa == 255` raw-store
path, where both CPU and kernel must return the SOURCE word untouched rather
than running it through the arithmetic (which would be lossy). `sa == 0` is
covered as the third early-out. NOT covered here: clipping, scaling and opacity
scaling of the same kernel -- those are the boundary/drawing/typed-upload specs.

## Verification guidance and troubleshooting
The sabotage that proves this file discriminates: change `dst_weight` back to
`inv` in the three channel numerators of `_glsl_blit()`, run
`sh scripts/tool/gen-blit-spirv.shs`, and re-run. The exact-value example goes
red reading `0x101F2F3F`; the `sa == 255` and `sa == 0` examples stay green,
which is exactly why an exact-value oracle was needed rather than a "blending
happened" smoke check. Restore and regenerate to return to green.

If the mirror example fails while the device example passes, the CPU oracle
moved and this file's literals are stale -- fix `color.spl` or the bug record,
never the literal alone.
"""
# Lifecycle: doc/08_tracking/bug/vulkan_glsl_blend_formula_stale_vs_cpu_2026-09-12.md
# @manual_section: vulkan_blend_cpu_parity
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/engine2d/backend_vulkan_glsl.spl 30%
# @cover src/lib/gc_async_mut/gpu/engine2d/backend_vulkan_spirv_raster_blobs.spl 2%
# @tag: unit, gpu, engine2d, vulkan, blend

use std.spec.*
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.engine2d.engine.{Engine2D}
use std.gc_async_mut.gpu.engine2d.color.{blend}

val FB: i32 = 32
val AT_X: i32 = 5
val AT_Y: i32 = 7

# The frozen triple. dst alpha is 0x10, NOT 0xFF -- the stale and frozen
# formulas agree whenever the destination is opaque, so an opaque ground would
# make this whole file vacuous.
val GROUND: u32 = 0x10203040u32
val SRC_TRANSLUCENT: u32 = 0x01020304u32
val EXPECT_TRANSLUCENT: u32 = 0x101E2D3Cu32
val SRC_OPAQUE: u32 = 0xFF020304u32
val SRC_INVISIBLE: u32 = 0x00FFFFFFu32

fn one_pixel(c: u32) -> [u32]:
    var out: [u32] = [0u32; 1]
    out[0] = c
    out

fn blended_at(src: u32) -> u32:
    """Composite one src-over pixel on the device and read it back."""
    val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
    e.clear(GROUND)
    e.draw_image_blend(AT_X, AT_Y, 1i32, 1i32, one_pixel(src))
    e.finalize_compute_frame_no_readback()
    val px = e.read_pixels_with_source().pixels
    e.shutdown()
    px[AT_Y.to_i64() * FB.to_i64() + AT_X.to_i64()]

describe "The Vulkan src-over kernel computes the frozen CPU blend exactly":

    it "runs on a real Vulkan device, and fails rather than skipping when it cannot":
        # PRECONDITION. Every claim below is about DEVICE pixels, and the host
        # fallback for draw_image_blend literally calls the CPU `blend()` this
        # file compares against -- so a skip would prove nothing at all.
        val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
        val name = e.backend_name()
        e.shutdown()
        expect(name).to_equal("vulkan")

    it "blends 0x01020304 over 0x10203040 to exactly 0x101E2D3C on the device":
        # REPRODUCING, exact value. dst_weight = (0x10 * 254) / 255 = 15,
        # out_a = 1 + 15 = 16, and each channel is (s*1 + d*15) / 16 ->
        # (30, 45, 60). The stale pre-unpremultiply formula yields 0x101F2F3F
        # here; every channel differs, so a partial regression is still visible.
        expect(blended_at(SRC_TRANSLUCENT)).to_equal(EXPECT_TRANSLUCENT)

    it "agrees with the frozen CPU blend() for that same pair":
        # MIRROR. Pinning the literal alone would let the CPU oracle drift away
        # from the device without anything going red; pinning only the mirror
        # would let BOTH drift together. Both claims are needed.
        expect(blend(SRC_TRANSLUCENT, GROUND)).to_equal(EXPECT_TRANSLUCENT)
        expect(blended_at(SRC_TRANSLUCENT)).to_equal(blend(SRC_TRANSLUCENT, GROUND))

    it "stores an opaque source word raw instead of running it through the arithmetic":
        # BOUNDARY, sa == 255. Both CPU and kernel take an early-out here and
        # must return the SOURCE untouched. Routing 0xFF020304 through the
        # arithmetic instead would give out_a = 255 + 0 and unchanged channels
        # by luck -- so the discriminating part is that the channels survive
        # EXACTLY, with dst = 0x10203040 contributing nothing.
        expect(blended_at(SRC_OPAQUE)).to_equal(SRC_OPAQUE)
        expect(blend(SRC_OPAQUE, GROUND)).to_equal(SRC_OPAQUE)

    it "leaves the destination untouched for a fully transparent source":
        # BOUNDARY, sa == 0. The kernel returns before touching fb; the CPU
        # returns dst. This is also the guard for out_a == 0, which is the only
        # way the channel division could divide by zero.
        expect(blended_at(SRC_INVISIBLE)).to_equal(GROUND)
        expect(blend(SRC_INVISIBLE, GROUND)).to_equal(GROUND)

    it "leaves the pixel next to the composite on the untouched ground":
        # BOUNDARY in space. A kernel that wrote one pixel too many would be
        # invisible to every example above, all of which read one address.
        val e = Engine2D.create_with_backend_fast(FB, FB, "vulkan")
        e.clear(GROUND)
        e.draw_image_blend(AT_X, AT_Y, 1i32, 1i32, one_pixel(SRC_TRANSLUCENT))
        e.finalize_compute_frame_no_readback()
        val px = e.read_pixels_with_source().pixels
        e.shutdown()
        expect(px[AT_Y.to_i64() * FB.to_i64() + AT_X.to_i64() + 1]).to_equal(GROUND)
